<section class="three-tool-section" id="juz-guide-definition"><h3>What is a Juz in the Quran?</h3><p>A Juz is one of 30 conventional reading sections of the Quran. The singular is Juz; plural Ajza. In South Asian usage, a part is often called a Para. Dividing recitation into thirty portions can support structured reading, especially during Ramadan, but a Juz is not necessarily a thematic unit or a new Surah. The Quran has 114 Surahs; a Juz may begin part-way through one.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="juz-guide-boundaries"><h3>Where do the 30 Juz begin and end?</h3><p>Each entry lists the first Surah and Ayah of a Juz, plus the first reference of the <em>next</em> Juz. That next boundary is exclusive: for example, Juz 1 begins at 1:1 and continues until just before 2:142, where Juz 2 begins. Juz 30 starts at 78:1 and runs through the last verse of Surah An-Nas, <strong>114:6</strong>. The table avoids inventing an exact final verse where another Surah ends and does not translate verse boundaries into page counts.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="juz-guide-steps"><h3>How to use the Juz finder step by step</h3><ol><li>Search by Juz number, Para number, name of the Surah where the Juz begins, or a reference such as 2:142.</li><li>Select a Juz from the thirty-entry list.</li><li>Read its beginning and the boundary at which the following Juz starts.</li><li>Open the linked Quran.com verse to begin reading the actual Quran.</li><li>Print or export the full CSV index for a personal reading plan.</li></ol><p>A Juz number and Surah number are different references: Juz 30 starts at Surah 78, not Surah 30.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="juz-guide-pages"><h3>Are Juz always twenty pages long?</h3><p>In the familiar 604-page Madani Mushaf, many Juz correspond roughly to twenty pages, but the opening pages and edition-specific pagination mean that an unqualified 'twenty pages per Juz' rule should not be used to locate a boundary. Indo-Pak scripts and other printed editions can use different page layouts. <a href="/tools/quran-planner/">Quran Planner</a> uses user-specified page counts; this finder uses Surah and Ayah references.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="juz-guide-example"><h3>Worked example: finding Juz 30 (Amma)</h3><div class="three-tool-example"><p>Search for <strong>Juz 30</strong>. The selected part begins at <strong>Surah An-Naba, 78:1</strong> and finishes at <strong>Surah An-Nas, 114:6</strong>. It includes many short Surahs often studied for Salah and Hifz. Open the external link to begin at Quran 78:1.</p><p>Searching for <strong>2:142</strong> instead locates the beginning of Juz 2 in Surah Al-Baqarah. These references describe boundaries, not the number of pages a particular Mushaf devotes to them.</p></div></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="juz-guide-ramadan"><h3>How to divide Quran reading across Ramadan</h3><p>One Juz per day creates a thirty-part reading goal. If the month has 29 days, a fixed thirty-day schedule needs an adjustment. Readers may divide a Juz into Hizb subdivisions, sessions, or page ranges depending on their edition. Reading attentively and consistently matters more than promising an unsupported obligatory pace. See <a href="https://quran.com/73/4">Quran 73:4</a> on measured recitation and the <a href="/tools/quran-planner/">reading-plan calculator</a>.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="juz-guide-hizb"><h3>Juz, Hizb, Rub al-Hizb and Manzil explained</h3><p>A Juz is one of thirty major recitation parts. A Hizb commonly denotes half a Juz, while smaller markers such as Rub al-Hizb help portion reading into shorter sessions. Other regional systems such as Manzil may divide the Quran differently. These navigation markers are reading aids rather than new revealed chapter boundaries.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="juz-guide-limits"><h3>Accuracy of verse boundaries, translations and recitation</h3><p>The list follows a conventional thirty-Juz verse-start index. It is a reference directory, not a substitute for a reliable Quran text or teacher. Check any specialised edition or alternative reading division against a trusted Mushaf. The page does not reproduce a translation, and no automatic Tajwid ruling is inferred from an index.</p></section>
